 ],
 "background": [
  {
   "text": "An upset is an undesired aircraft state characterized by unintentional divergences from parameters normally experienced during operations. It may involve pitch and/or bank angle divergences and may lead to inappropriate airspeeds for the conditions.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 28/32",
     "pdf": 473
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Prevention relies on effective monitoring of the environment, the aircraft energy state, the flight path and the technical state (flight control laws, system failures). All flight crew members are responsible for it.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 28/32",
     "pdf": 473
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "When the abnormal attitude law engages: pitch alternate law with no protection (no load factor limitation), roll direct law, yaw mechanical law. Autotrim is not available, so USE MAN PITCH TRIM is displayed on the PFDs, and F/CTL DIRECT LAW is displayed on the ECAM.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 18/20",
     "pdf": 1629
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Back within the normal flight envelope, these conditions remain for the remainder of the flight: pitch alternate law without reduced protection, roll direct law, yaw alternate law, and F/CTL ALTN LAW on the ECAM.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 18/20",
     "pdf": 1629
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "technique": [
  {
   "text": "The nose high and nose low techniques are a logical progression and are not necessarily procedural. The sequence is guidance only: a series of options to consider depending on the situation. The crew may apply these actions or part of them, mainly if the recovery is effective.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 30/32",
     "pdf": 475
    },
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 608
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Troubleshooting the cause of the upset is secondary to initiating the recovery, but the pilot must still recognize and confirm the situation first.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 611
    },
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 29/32",
     "pdf": 474
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "All upset recovery techniques assume the aircraft is not stalled. When needed, recover from the stall first.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 608
    },
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 29/32",
     "pdf": 474
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The PFD is a primary reference for recovery. Even in extreme attitudes, some portion of the sky or ground indications is present on the pitch scale, and the bank indicator gives the aircraft bank.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-MISC",
     "page": "P 29/32",
     "pdf": 474
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Avoid exaggerated control inputs through reflex responses. Inputs must be smooth, positive and proportional to the amount and rate of pitch, roll or yaw experienced.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 608
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Aggressive and opposite flight control inputs must not be applied: they can lead to loads higher than the limit and result in structural damage or failure. If you sense an over control, set the sidestick to the neutral position.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 574
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The rudder should not be used to induce roll, or to counter roll induced by any type of turbulence.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "PDP",
     "code": "Unreliable speed indication (and UPRT)",
     "page": "XFTDA6UNRASPD01",
     "pdf": 575
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
